          "title": "Rigorous Step-by-Step Construction of Myers' Blended Index",
          "question": "To analyze digit preference across all ten digits $0, 1, 2, \\dots, 9$, a demographic research institute extracts single-year age data from a provincial census covering ages 10 through 89. The unweighted sums of populations starting at age 10 ($U_d = \\sum_{k=1}^7 P_{10k+d}$) and starting at age 20 ($V_d = \\sum_{k=2}^8 P_{10k+d}$) for each terminal digit $d$ are obtained as follows:\n\n| Terminal Digit ($d$) | $U_d$ (Ages 10–79) | $V_d$ (Ages 20–89) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **0** | $542{,}000$ | $468{,}000$ |\n| **1** | $215{,}000$ | $176{,}000$ |\n| **2** | $328{,}000$ | $271{,}000$ |\n| **3** | $234{,}000$ | $191{,}000$ |\n| **4** | $256{,}000$ | $208{,}000$ |\n| **5** | $482{,}000$ | $398{,}000$ |\n| **6** | $268{,}000$ | $217{,}000$ |\n| **7** | $221{,}000$ | $178{,}000$ |\n| **8** | $341{,}000$ | $279{,}000$ |\n| **9** | $198{,}000$ | $159{,}000$ |\n\n1. Apply Myers' linear blending coefficients $(d+1)$ and $(9-d)$ to compute the blended population $B_d$ for each terminal digit $d \\in \\{0, 1, \\dots, 9\\}$.\n2. Calculate the total blended population $B_{\\text{total}}$ and the percentage share $p_d$ of each terminal digit.\n3. Compute the absolute deviations from the $10.0\\%$ theoretical uniform expectation, determine Myers' Blended Index $M$, and provide a complete diagnostic assessment of digit attraction and digit avoidance.",
